## memory-canary (chassis-shipped, default-on)

Daily write-read-verify against the memory MCP knowledge graph. Writes a nonce
to the reserved entity `health:memory-canary`, then reads it back in a
**separate** server invocation and asserts the two match. Fires only on
mismatch, error, or an empty read.

The failure it exists for: on the V1 reference install the graph was unreachable from
inside the container for at least sixteen days (2026-07-24 to 2026-08-09)
because `.mcp.json` baked an absolute host path that does not exist in the
container namespace. Reads returned a well-formed empty graph, which reads as
"no prior context" rather than "broken tool", so nothing alerted. Fixed in
#142 / #143 - this is the detector for it.

Deliberately NOT an mtime staleness threshold: memory writes are event-driven,
so a quiet week is indistinguishable from a broken graph, and a threshold loose
enough to avoid false alarms would not have caught the outage until late.

`criticality: critical` - a silently amnesiac assistant is not a background
concern, and the check must keep running in conservation mode.

Costs three short-lived `npx @modelcontextprotocol/server-memory` spawns per
run (clear, write, read - one tool call each), about 1.7s warm, which is why it
is daily rather than every 15m.

Issue: scrollinondubs/behalfbot#145. Path shape: scrollinondubs/behalfbot#151
(the registration below used `${CHASSIS_HOME}/chassis/...`, which resolves on
no containerized install; see "Containerized installs" under Path conventions).

```yaml
schedule: daily 07:00
gather: state/chassis-root/scripts/gather-memory-canary.sh
condition: threshold count > 0
prompt: state/chassis-root/scheduled-tasks/memory-canary-prompt.md
model: sonnet
budget: 1
criticality: critical
```

